#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Two checks over the settings registry:
#
#   1. Fail if a settings group is registered in production but not in the test
#      harness that is supposed to mirror it.
#   2. Fail if two settings declare the same `toml_path` or the same
#      `storage_key`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (check 1)
# --------------------------
# PR #480 went red on nextest with:
#
#     Cannot get singleton model of type
#     "warp::settings::local_control::LocalControlSettings" that was never
#     registered
#
# `app/src/settings/init.rs`'s `register_all_settings` is the production list
# of every settings group the app registers with the `SettingsManager` at
# startup. `app/src/test_util/settings.rs`'s
# `initialize_settings_for_tests_with_mode` is a second, hand-maintained copy
# of that same list, built for tests that need a populated App context
# without booting the whole app. The two are supposed to stay in lockstep,
# but nothing enforces that: `LocalControlSettings::register` was added to
# production in PR #472 (which added three settings groups) and the test
# harness silently fell behind. It compiled fine and passed every test that
# didn't happen to touch `LocalControlSettings` -- until one finally did,
# months later, and the failure landed on an unrelated PR.
#
# WHY THE COMPILER CANNOT CATCH THIS
# -----------------------------------
# `register(ctx)` populates a runtime registry keyed by `TypeId`; nothing
# about a missing call site fails to type-check. The `SettingsManager` panics
# only when something later asks for a singleton that was never inserted --
# at runtime, in whichever test happens to be first to drive that code path.
# That is exactly the "compiles fine, blows up on whoever touches it next"
# shape the stub-coverage guard exists for, one function over.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (check 2)
# --------------------------
# Issue #635: `warpify.ssh.enable_legacy_ssh_wrapper` was declared TWICE --
# in `SshSettings` (`app/src/settings/ssh.rs`) and in `WarpifySettings`
# (`app/src/terminal/warpify/settings.rs`) -- against the same `toml_path`
# AND the same `storage_key` ("EnableSSHWrapper"). That is not two settings.
# `SettingsManager::register_setting` (`crates/settings/src/manager.rs`)
# keys all six of its maps by storage key with `HashMap::insert`, so the
# group registered second in `register_all_settings` silently evicted the
# first's entry. Two things follow. The `update_fns`/`load_fns`/`clear_fns`
# a TOML reload or settings-file load dispatches were bound to the losing
# group's model, which therefore never saw an update; and `inventory` still
# held BOTH declarations, so the schema and default-settings generators
# emitted the key twice.
#
# The eviction also swapped the registered `SyncToCloud` (`Never` ->
# `Globally(Yes)`), which in upstream's design would defeat the
# warpdotdev/Warp#13228 guard on that migration -- and the test asserting
# `EnableSshWrapper::sync_to_cloud() == Never` would not have noticed, since
# it asks the *type*, not the registry. State that conditionally: it could
# not happen in this fork, which has no `CloudPreferencesSyncer` (see
# `DECLINED.md`) and so consumes `sync_to_cloud` nowhere but the settings
# UI's local-only icon. The defect is real on the two grounds above; the
# sync loop is upstream's.
#
# This is the same shape as check 1 one level down: a registry keyed by
# something the compiler does not police, populated by `insert`, where the
# loser is not an error but an absence. Check 1 catches a group that never
# registers; check 2 catches a setting whose registration is thrown away.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
# ------------
# Extract the brace-balanced body of `register_all_settings` in
# `app/src/settings/init.rs` and of `initialize_settings_for_tests_with_mode`
# in `app/src/test_util/settings.rs`, then pull every `<Name>::register(...)`
# / `<Name>::register_and_subscribe_to_events(...)` call out of each (as a
# direct call or as a bare function reference, e.g.
# `app.update(AISettings::register_and_subscribe_to_events)`). This is
# text-based, not `cfg`-aware -- a group registered behind
# `#[cfg(feature = "local_fs")]` in both files still counts as present in
# both, same approximation the other two guards make deliberately (see their
# header comments).
#
# Check 2 walks every non-test `.rs` file under `app/src` and `crates/*/src`
# with a small awk state machine: `define_settings_group!` entries,
# `maybe_define_setting!` and `implement_setting_for_enum!` each name a
# setting, and the `toml_path:` / `storage_key:` string literals that follow
# belong to it. A setting with no explicit `storage_key:` gets the setting
# type's own name, which is what `define_setting!`'s convenience arms pass
# (`storage_key: stringify!($name)`).
#
# Those three are the only declaration forms in the tree today. A *direct*
# `define_setting!` invocation is invisible to this awk -- there are zero such
# sites outside `crates/settings/src/macros.rs`, and the omission fails safe
# (a missed declaration cannot manufacture a duplicate, only miss one), but do
# not read the list above as exhaustive if that ever changes.
#
# The test exclusion is by FILENAME, not directory, so a test file that does
# not end in `_test.rs`/`_tests.rs` is scanned -- `crates/integration/src/test/**`
# is the live example. Harmless today (nothing there declares a setting) and
# arguably right, since those are not settings fixtures.
#
# `crates/settings/src/macros.rs` is skipped because it *defines* those macros:
# its `macro_rules!` arms and its doc examples are not declarations. Test files
# (`*_test.rs`, `*_tests.rs`, `*test_support*`) are excluded on purpose:
# `crates/settings`'s own fixtures declare `test.simple_setting` twice, in
# `macros_tests.rs` and `mod_tests.rs`, and that is fine -- they are separate
# fixtures in one test binary, not two declarations of one shipped setting.
#
# NO ESCAPE HATCH, DELIBERATELY. There is no legitimate aliasing case in this
# tree: across the 303 settings this finds, the only duplicate of either key
# was #635's, and it was a defect. A deliberate alias cannot work anyway -- the second
# declaration does not alias the first, it evicts it from the registry. If a
# real case ever turns up, add the exemption here with the reason, not a
# blanket skip.
#
# `app/src/settings/init_tests.rs`'s `no_two_settings_declare_the_same_toml_path`
# makes the same assertion at runtime over the `inventory` registry that
# actually linked. This one is the fast pre-push half: it parses source and
# compiles nothing, and it also covers explicit `storage_key:` collisions,
# which the schema entry does not carry.
#
# DIRECTION: production -> test is a hard failure. test -> production is not
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------
# A group in production but missing from the test harness is exactly PR
# #480's crash: any test that reaches it panics. That direction fails this
# check.
#
# A group registered ONLY in the test harness does not crash anything --
# it is either legitimate test-only scaffolding (a mock or seam that has no
# production registration path at all) or a stale leftover from a group that
# was removed from production, which is harmless dead weight rather than a
# live bug. Failing the build on it would mean any deliberate test double has
# to fight this guard. So it is reported as an informational note, never a
# failure -- the same asymmetry `check_cloud_boundary` uses for its
# "allowlist entries no longer present" case.
#
# This compares the CURRENT relationship between the two files, not a
# hardcoded list -- it passes whether or not any particular fix (e.g. PR
# #480's `LocalControlSettings::register(app);` addition) has landed yet.
# LC_ALL=C on sorts is deliberate: under a UTF-8 locale `sort` ignores
# punctuation when collating, so tokens differing only by a leading '#'
# (or similar) compare EQUAL and `sort -u` silently drops one. That made
# check_sweep_ledger pass locally and fail in CI on 2026-08-19.

set -uo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")/.."

PROD_FILE="app/src/settings/init.rs"
PROD_FN="register_all_settings"
TEST_FILE="app/src/test_util/settings.rs"
TEST_FN="initialize_settings_for_tests_with_mode"

for f in "$PROD_FILE" "$TEST_FILE"; do
    if [[ ! -f "$f" ]]; then
        echo "error: $f is missing" >&2
        exit 2
    fi
done

# Print the brace-balanced body of `fn <fn>(...) { ... }`, starting from the
# line containing the signature and ending at the line whose closing brace
# brings the depth back to zero. Handles multi-line signatures (the test
# harness's does) because it only starts counting braces once it starts
# capturing, and only treats depth 0 as "done" after depth has gone positive
# at least once.
extract_fn_body() {
    local file="$1" fn="$2"
    awk -v fn="$fn" '
        !capturing && $0 ~ ("fn[ \t]+" fn "\\(") { capturing = 1 }
        capturing {
            print
            depth += gsub(/\{/, "{")
            depth -= gsub(/\}/, "}")
            if (started && depth == 0) { exit }
            if (depth > 0) { started = 1 }
        }
    ' "$file"
}

# Pull the settings-group type name out of every `<Name>::register(...)` or
# `<Name>::register_and_subscribe_to_events` occurrence (call or bare
# function reference). The trailing `\b` keeps this from matching unrelated
# methods that merely start with "register", e.g.
# `secure_storage::register_noop`.
extract_register_calls() {
    grep -oE '[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*::(register|register_and_subscribe_to_events)\b' \
        | sed -E 's/::(register|register_and_subscribe_to_events)$//' \
        | LC_ALL=C sort -u
}

prod_body="$(extract_fn_body "$PROD_FILE" "$PROD_FN")"
test_body="$(extract_fn_body "$TEST_FILE" "$TEST_FN")"

if [[ -z "$prod_body" ]]; then
    echo "error: could not find fn $PROD_FN in $PROD_FILE (extraction is out of date)" >&2
    exit 2
fi
if [[ -z "$test_body" ]]; then
    echo "error: could not find fn $TEST_FN in $TEST_FILE (extraction is out of date)" >&2
    exit 2
fi

prod_set="$(mktemp)"
test_set="$(mktemp)"
printf '%s\n' "$prod_body" | extract_register_calls > "$prod_set"
printf '%s\n' "$test_body" | extract_register_calls > "$test_set"

missing="$(comm -23 "$prod_set" "$test_set")"
extra="$(comm -13 "$prod_set" "$test_set")"

status=0

if [[ -n "$missing" ]]; then
    status=1
    echo "error: settings group(s) registered in production but not in the test harness:" >&2
    echo "$missing" | sed 's/^/  - /' >&2
    cat >&2 <<EOF

$PROD_FN ($PROD_FILE) registers these, but $TEST_FN
($TEST_FILE) does not. This is the PR #480 shape: any test
that drives one of these groups through the real App registry panics with
"Cannot get singleton model of type ... that was never registered", in
whichever test happens to exercise it first -- which can be long after the
group was added to production.

Fix: add the matching "<Name>::register(app);" call to
$TEST_FN in $TEST_FILE.
EOF
fi

if [[ -n "$extra" ]]; then
    # Not a failure -- see header comment for why.
    echo "note: settings group(s) registered only in the test harness (not in production):"
    echo "$extra" | sed 's/^/  - /'
fi

group_count="$(wc -l < "$prod_set" | tr -d ' ')"
rm -f "$prod_set" "$test_set"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Check 2: no two settings declare the same toml_path or the same storage_key.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Emit one "<kind>\t<key>\t<file>:<line>" record per declared setting: a
# toml_path record when the setting has one, and always a storage_key record
# (the explicit `storage_key:` if given, otherwise the setting type's own name,
# which is what `define_setting!` passes as `stringify!($name)`).
# `read -d ''` returns non-zero when it hits EOF instead of the delimiter,
# which is exactly what a heredoc does; `|| true` keeps that off `$?`.
read -r -d '' EXTRACT_SETTINGS_AWK <<'AWK' || true
function flush() {
    if (name == "") return
    # Anything that is not a bare Rust identifier came from a macro_rules! arm
    # pattern, not a declaration.
    if (name !~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$/) { name = ""; path = ""; key = ""; return }
    if (path != "") print "toml_path\t" path "\t" namefile ":" nameline
    print "storage_key\t" (key != "" ? key : name) "\t" namefile ":" nameline
    name = ""; path = ""; key = ""
}
FNR == 1 { flush(); in_group = 0; pending_enum = 0 }
{
    # Strip line comments (this also removes `///` and `//!` doc lines) and skip
    # block-comment continuations, so the macro examples in the settings crate's
    # own docs are not mistaken for declarations.
    line = $0
    sub(/[ \t]*\/\/.*$/, "", line)
    if (line ~ /^[ \t]*\*/) next
}
# A top-level item closed at column 0 ends whatever declaration was open.
line ~ /^[)}]/ { flush(); in_group = 0; next }
line ~ /define_settings_group![ \t]*\(/ { flush(); in_group = 1; next }
line ~ /maybe_define_setting![ \t]*\(/ {
    flush()
    if (match(line, /maybe_define_setting![ \t]*\([ \t]*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/)) {
        s = substr(line, RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/.*\(/, "", s); gsub(/[ \t]/, "", s)
        name = s; namefile = FILENAME; nameline = FNR
    }
    next
}
line ~ /implement_setting_for_enum![ \t]*\(/ { flush(); pending_enum = 1; next }
# The enum's name is the first argument, on the line after the macro call.
pending_enum && line ~ /[A-Za-z_]/ {
    s = line; gsub(/[ \t,]/, "", s)
    name = s; namefile = FILENAME; nameline = FNR; pending_enum = 0
    next
}
# A `field: SettingType { ... }` entry inside a settings group.
in_group && line ~ /^[ \t]*[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*[ \t]*:[ \t]*[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9_]*[ \t]*\{/ {
    flush()
    s = line; sub(/^[ \t]*[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*[ \t]*:[ \t]*/, "", s); sub(/[ \t]*\{.*$/, "", s)
    name = s; namefile = FILENAME; nameline = FNR
    next
}
name != "" && line ~ /^[ \t]*storage_key[ \t]*:[ \t]*"/ {
    s = line; sub(/^[^"]*"/, "", s); sub(/".*$/, "", s); key = s; next
}
name != "" && line ~ /^[ \t]*toml_path[ \t]*:[ \t]*"/ {
    s = line; sub(/^[^"]*"/, "", s); sub(/".*$/, "", s); path = s; next
}
END { flush() }
AWK

decls="$(mktemp)"
find app/src crates/*/src -name '*.rs' \
     ! -name '*_test.rs' ! -name '*_tests.rs' ! -name '*test_support*' \
     ! -path 'crates/settings/src/macros.rs' -print0 \
    | xargs -0 awk "$EXTRACT_SETTINGS_AWK" > "$decls"

if [[ ! -s "$decls" ]]; then
    echo "error: found no setting declarations at all (extraction is out of date)" >&2
    rm -f "$decls"
    exit 2
fi

# LC_ALL=C on every sort here for the reason in the header: a UTF-8 collation
# ignores punctuation, so `warpify.ssh.x` and `warpifysshx` compare EQUAL and
# `uniq -d` misses the pair -- and settings keys are all punctuation.
report_duplicates() {
    local kind="$1" dupes
    dupes="$(awk -F'\t' -v k="$kind" '$1 == k { print $2 }' "$decls" \
             | LC_ALL=C sort | LC_ALL=C uniq -d)"
    [[ -z "$dupes" ]] && return 0

    echo "error: duplicate settings $kind:" >&2
    while IFS= read -r dupe; do
        echo "  $dupe" >&2
        # Sort by file, then by line NUMERICALLY -- a plain sort puts :621 before
        # :86. LC_ALL=C for the reason above.
        awk -F'\t' -v k="$kind" -v d="$dupe" '$1 == k && $2 == d { print "      " $3 }' \
            "$decls" | LC_ALL=C sort -t: -k1,1 -k2,2n >&2
    done <<< "$dupes"
    return 1
}

dup_status=0
report_duplicates toml_path || dup_status=1
report_duplicates storage_key || dup_status=1

if [[ $dup_status -ne 0 ]]; then
    status=1
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'

A second declaration of a key does not create a second setting. Every
SettingsManager map is keyed by storage key and populated with
HashMap::insert, so the settings group registered LAST in
register_all_settings silently evicts the other's entry. The loser then has
no registration at all: the update/load/clear callbacks a TOML reload
dispatches go to the winner's model only, while both groups still read and
write the one underlying value. The schema and default-settings generators
meanwhile see both declarations via inventory and emit the key twice. The
registered sync_to_cloud, default and supported_platforms are the winner's.
That is issue #635.

Fix: delete one declaration and point its readers at the survivor. If the
two really are meant to be the same setting, that is what deleting one
gives you; if they are meant to be different settings, give one a different
toml_path and storage_key.
EOF
fi

setting_count="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "storage_key"' "$decls" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
rm -f "$decls"

if [[ $status -eq 0 && -z "$extra" ]]; then
    echo "settings registry: ok ($group_count group(s) registered in both, $setting_count setting(s) with unique toml_path and storage_key)"
fi

exit $status
